    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-1405: the cross product is (10−28,16+3,−21−40)=⟨−18,19,−61⟩. q-19779: it is (0−15,6−3,5−0)=⟨−15,3,5⟩. q-135315 and q-135350: invert each nonzero diagonal entry; 1/√5=√5/5. q-135366 and q-135367: permutation matrices have inverses equal to their transposes. q-135396 and q-135387: write A=I+N, where N²=0, so A⁻¹=I−N. q-160771: t≥−2 already excludes the denominator's zero at −3. q-162673: only the square root restricts the domain, to t≥−1. q-160773: intersect −2≤t≤2 with t>1 to obtain (1,2]. q-162656: 3−s>0 requires s<3. q-162632 and q-162637: secant and tangent exclude exactly π/2+nπ; the other components are defined for every real t. q-32091: the lines intersect once and the coefficient matrix is invertible; its determinant is 9+2√0.7>0, so I and III hold. q-127919: a unique solution for a square system implies a trivial nullspace, invertibility, and a nonzero determinant, so I and II hold. The last two source explanations describe the premise as uniqueness for every right-hand side; the captured premise states uniqueness for the given system, but the stronger property follows from invertibility, so their final answers remain correct.",
